    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "## El Problema\n\nThe following issues compounded to create a confusing, arguably broken, and certainly unclear state of the directory browser currently. This has resulted in me frantically opening ~6-7 open prs. This is not ideal.\n\nI take complete responsibility for all of these failures.\n \n1. Code-level issues: the contract for **\"interacting\" (enable, disable, hook into)** with the plugin was not made clear _before_ implementation. Related: #40689, nvim-mini/mini.nvim/pull/2493 \n\n2. Doc-level issues: [Breaking changes](#40552) were made without warning. Docs of how to (dynamically) delete the plugin are unclear. [This erroneous condition](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/pull/40695/changes#diff-ddde6eb58e360a5e40cec2d0c3e175a5d2fb694fb51a33fa897e806d0c7c98c0R3256) was also a naive skill issue on my end.\n\n3. Poor downstream foresight: the compatibility of nightly neovim with mini.files, oil, canola, etc. was not explicitly verified by myself.\n\n## La Soluzione\n\n1. Discuss here\n2. Revise git history and mark the change in directory naming as breaking\n3. Add a comprehensive suite for a custom file manager that runs through all the discovered pain points once item 1 is decided on.\n\n## My Opinion\n\nConsider the following ideas which seem initially disjoint:\n\n1. **Disabling the plugin**: Using `vim.g.loaded_nvim_dir_plugin` to disable dir.lua _on startup only_ follows precedent and should be kept. It should not dynamically enable/disable the plugin.\n\n2. **Dynamically enabling/disabling the plugin**: Like netrw, dir.lua is enabled by default. **The only plausible reason to disable it dynamically would be to replace it with another file manager** (see [this comment](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/pull/2493#issuecomment-4948447017)).\n- **dir.lua autocmd**: the existence/proposition of a dir.lua autocmd exists:\n\n  a) As a new future-proof way to dynamically disable nvim plugins \n  b) To match the existing `FileExplorer` autocmd from vim, which existed for the purpose of point 1 in vim.\n\n3. There (AFAICT) are no established patterns of dynamically disabling/enabling builtin plugins right now.\n4. A plugin ported from vim to neovim has no inherent responsibility to exactly maintain it's behavior, but rather reasonably repurpose it for the goals of neovim (i.e. exploit the opportunity to improve design)\n5. Sometimes, Point 5 warrants behavior changes that impact downstream plugins (in this case, file managers).\n6. dir.lua must easily be replaceable\n\nI arrive at the following opinion: **There should be no way to dynamically enable/disable dir.lua; hijacking into an API surface is preferred.**\n\nBecause of point 1, 2, and 5, *we have* to provide a way for people to dynamically supplant dir.lua with their preferred file manager. Point 2a, 2b, 3, and 4 encourage not solving dynamic disabling now because vim's netrw is the _only time this is ever done_ - and just because it was done this way in vim, that doesn't mean we should do it here. Providing an autocmd `FileType directory` where one synchronously hooks into a callback seems like the most convincing approach right now, covering Point 6. \n\nOpen to new ideas and approaches - this is just my take.",
