        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Calling this even remotely close to post-mortem is too much indeed. A bit more consideration about how to disable the `dir.lua` behavior prior to merging this as opt-out would have saved some collective time and effort of reading 5-6 different PRs. But `master` is indeed for (reasonable) experimentation, so that's understandable.\n\nTo recap, in short my expectation is that there needs to be a way to *do something* to opt-out of `dir.lua` populating lines of directory buffer *regardless of when that something is done*.\n\n> 3\\. There (AFAICT) are no established patterns of dynamically disabling/enabling builtin plugins right now.\n\nThere is a `netrw` approach of disabling its behavior by deleting a specific augroup. Yes, this is clunky, but if documented explicitly and respected in the future (no name change), this works fine as a one-time investment for a file explorer plugin author.\n\n> i've lost track of why we can't support that with dir.lua, but i think it's because of my hope/insistence on using `filetype=directory` \"somehow\" as the central interface for deciding dir-buffer presentation.\n\nI think deleting augroup is fine. The one possible downside is that it disables the plugin's behavior for the whole session, i.e. not intended to be reverted. This seems fine.\n\nA more dynamic way of disabling plugin's behavior is also fine. Like by third-party plugin manager to create custom `au FileType directory ...` autocommand and do some buffer-local action in it. For example, setting `vim.b.nvim_dir_disable = true`, `vim.b.directory_provider = 'other'`, or even `vim.bo.filetype = 'my-directory'`. The `dir.lua` autocommand callback can then check the condition and stop early if it holds. The usual problem here is to make sure that the autocommand order does interfere here, i.e. this has effect regardless of when third-part plugin creates its autocommand: during or after startup.",
