        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "stepping back and thinking through, the path to victory still seems clear. here's my chain of thought (yes, humans still have that :p):\n\n1. in the olden times, netrw defined an augroup, and 3P dir browsers declared their intention by deleting its augroup.\n2. now we have `filetype.lua` which recognizes dir bufs and sets `filetype=directory`\n3. the open question is how dir.lua, and 3P dir browsers, can decide who handles that.\n\nthis approach seems to address all problems:\n \n1. `runtime/plugin/dir.lua` handles `FileType directory` event (augroup: `nvim.dir`, idk)\n    - drop all other ways of handling dir bufs. let `filetype.lua` be the authority.\n2. 3P dir browsers can:\n    1. delete the `nvim.dir` augroup\n    2. handle `FileType directory`\n\ni'm ignoring the \"default - mapping\", that's a separate problem (right).\n\nwhat am i missing? (in one sentence, not a paragraph please)",
            "created_at": "2026-07-12T13:23:09Z",
